WEBVTT

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.292
Welcome back to your How We Major Mother podcast feed.
欢迎回到您的“我们如何主要母亲”播客源。

00:00:03.292 --> 00:00:18.020
Today, we're presenting an appearance by our own Josh Radner on a podcast called Arts Educators Save the World, where successful artists sit in conversation with their mentors to talk about the importance of art, arts education and mentorship.
今天，我们将在一个名为“艺术教育者拯救世界”的播客中介绍我们自己的乔什·拉德纳 (Josh Radner)，其中成功的艺术家与他们的导师坐在一起交谈，谈论艺术、艺术教育和指导的重要性。

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:27.019
I produce this podcast with my lifelong friend Erica, and this is a fantastic episode with Josh and one of his teachers from NYU, Deborah Lapidus.
我和我一生的朋友艾丽卡一起制作了这个播客，这是与乔什和他来自纽约大学的一位老师黛博拉·拉皮德斯一起制作的精彩剧集。

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:42.184
And next week, we've got another episode of Arts Educators Save the World for you in our bonus slot, this time with Craig Thomas, in conversation with Rob Greenberg, who worked with Craig and Carter on How I Met Your Mother, as, to quote Craig, the adult in the room.
下周，我们将在额外时段为您提供另一集《艺术教育家拯救世界》，这一次是克雷格·托马斯与罗布·格林伯格的对话，罗布·格林伯格曾与克雷格和卡特合作过《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，正如房间里的成年人克雷格所说。

00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:56.099
If you enjoy these episodes, please check out Arts Educators Save the World where you get podcasts or guests have included Lin-Manuel Miranda, Annaly Ashford, Cecily Strong, Bradley Whitford, Jonathan Groff, Billy Eichner, and many, many others.
如果您喜欢这些剧集，请查看艺术教育家拯救世界，您可以在其中获得播客或嘉宾，其中包括林-曼努尔·米兰达、安娜莉·阿什福德、塞西莉·斯特朗、布拉德利·惠特福德、乔纳森·格罗夫、比利·艾希纳等。

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.619
Enjoy.
享受。

00:00:57.619 --> 00:01:03.299
One of the great things about Deb is she's one of the funniest people I know and her humor was so disarming.
黛布最伟大的事情之一就是她是我认识的最有趣的人之一，她的幽默是如此令人放松。

00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:07.859
I remember being in her class and it didn't feel like I needed to decode anything.
我记得在她的课上，感觉我不需要解码任何东西。

00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:10.140
It felt like, oh, I know how to do this.
感觉就像，哦，我知道该怎么做。

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.620
We sing and we try to communicate the depth and text and meaning of a song.
我们唱歌并试图传达歌曲的深度、文本和含义。

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.700
There was just something clear and welcoming about her style and her class.
她的风格和她的班级有一些清晰和热情的东西。

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.680
I felt at home and I felt like I had a right to be there.
我有宾至如归的感觉，我觉得我有权利呆在那里。

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:33.140
This is Arts Educators Save the World, where successful artists and their mentors talk about how arts education transformed their lives.
这是艺术教育者拯救世界，成功的艺术家和他们的导师在这里谈论艺术教育如何改变他们的生活。

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:39.609
Hey!
嘿！

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:40.799
Welcome back.
欢迎回来。

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:46.959
I'm Erica Rosenfeld-Halverson, and here we are, back with more conversation around artists and mentorship.
我是艾丽卡·罗森菲尔德-哈尔弗森，我们回来了，我们将更多地围绕艺术家和指导进行对话。

00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:52.560
And I'm here again with my producer, the fabulous superhero sidekick, Alec Lev.
我又和我的制作人、出色的超级英雄搭档亚历克·列夫一起来到这里。

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.200
Hello, Alec.
你好，亚历克。

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.280
Hello, Erica.
你好，艾丽卡。

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:05.519
You've gotten to know Alec and I a little bit through our collective journey with Miss Ames and Bobby and Lynn, but here in this episode, we'll add another fun fact.
通过我们与艾姆斯小姐、鲍比和林恩的集体旅程，你已经对亚历克和我有了一些了解，但在本集中，我们将添加另一个有趣的事实。

00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:07.680
We were both theater majors in college.
我们都是大学戏剧专业的。

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.680
That's right.
这是正确的。

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.840
We both trained to be professional theater types.
我们都接受过专业戏剧培训。

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.240
We both have continued to work on the stage over the years.
多年来我们都继续在舞台上工作。

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:22.479
I have played everything from a giant bumblebee teacher to a Yiddish stage performer in the 1940s.
我扮演过各种角色，从巨型大黄蜂老师到 20 世纪 40 年代的意第绪语舞台表演者。

00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:29.159
My training was more as a director and also importantly during and after college, I became fluent in American Sign Language.
我的训练更多的是作为一名导演，而且重要的是，在大学期间和毕业后，我变得流利的美国手语。

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:36.560
So I performed in Children's Theater with the National Theater of the Deaf, and then I also acted with Deaf West Theater here in Los Angeles.
所以我和国家聋人剧院一起在儿童剧院表演，然后我也在洛杉矶的聋人西剧院一起表演。

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:45.960
But my real passion is for directing, and along with various stage shows over the years, next year I'll be co-directing an opera with a mixed cast of Deaf and Hearing performers.
但我真正的热情是导演，随着多年来的各种舞台表演，明年我将与聋哑和听力演员的混合演员共同导演一部歌剧。

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:55.080
And earlier this year in 2022, I wrote and directed a film called What, which is a black and white silent feature film again, starring Deaf and Hearing actors.
今年早些时候，即 2022 年，我编剧并导演了一部名为《What》的电影，这又是一部黑白无声故事片，由聋人和听力演员主演。

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:05.560
I also, and this is Jermaine to our upcoming talk, I can be seen for good 20 to 30 seconds on the episode best prom ever in season three of How I Met Your Mother.
我也是，我是我们即将进行的演讲中的杰梅因，我可以在《老爸老妈的浪漫史》第三季中最好的舞会剧集中出现 20 到 30 秒。

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.639
Which conveniently brings us to one of our guests this week, Josh Radner.
这很方便地让我们见到了本周的一位客人，乔什·拉德纳。

00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:15.639
He's an actor, a writer, a director, and a musician.
他是一名演员、作家、导演和音乐家。

00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:24.439
And when we invited him to be on the show, he wanted to talk about a teacher from his time at New York University's graduate program at Tisch School of the Arts.
当我们邀请他参加节目时，他想谈谈他在纽约大学蒂施艺术学院研究生项目时的一位老师。

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:29.520
And the woman he chose, Deb Lapidus, actually teaches singing to actors.
他选择的女人黛布·拉皮杜斯（Deb Lapidus）实际上是在教演员唱歌。

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.080
But as you'll see, her work is completely about storytelling and about life.
但正如你将看到的，她的作品完全是关于讲故事和生活。

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:43.199
In this conversation, I will say that Josh and Deb go very deep on singing and acting.
在这次谈话中，我会说乔什和黛布在歌唱和表演方面非常深入。

00:03:43.199 --> 00:03:53.960
And I think it's important as we listen to this, that we remember the heart of your book is that it doesn't matter if you're a singer or an actor or a painter or a sculptor.
我认为重要的是，当我们听到这个时，我们要记住你的书的核心是，无论你是歌手、演员、画家还是雕塑家，这并不重要。

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.120
That's not what the goal of this here.
这不是这里的目标。

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:22.639
It's saying that listen to the way, the relationship, that Deb and Josh created together as mentor students, how they listen to each other, how they worked off each other, and just imagine the magic that happens there happening in any class using some of the techniques of the artist and the art teacher to open you up to risk taking, to realizing that doing poorly on the math test is not failing.
它是说，倾听黛布和乔什作为导师学生共同创造的方式和关系，他们如何互相倾听，他们如何互相配合，想象一下在任何课堂上发生的魔法，使用艺术家和美术老师的一些技巧让你敢于冒险，意识到在数学考试中表现不佳并不意味着失败。

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:27.459
It's the step towards the day that you will get the right answer on the math test.
这是朝着您在数学考试中获得正确答案的那一天迈出的一步。

00:04:27.459 --> 00:04:45.879
And that good teaching of any kind puts scaffolds around that so that each student feels ready to take those risks, even if the kinds of supports that one person needs may not be the kind of supports that another person needs.
任何形式的良好教学都会为其提供支架，以便每个学生都准备好承担这些风险，即使一个人需要的支持可能不是另一个人需要的支持。

00:04:45.879 --> 00:05:00.720
And Deb and Josh talk about that too and talk about the kinds of scaffolds and supports that he needed, but they also talk about what that looked like for some of the other people who Josh was interacting with when he was in Deb's classroom.
Deb 和 Josh 也谈论了这一点，谈论了他需要的各种支架和支持，但他们也谈论了当 Josh 在 Deb 的教室里时与 Josh 互动的其他一些人的情况。

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:11.089
Let's listen.
我们来听听。

00:05:11.089 --> 00:05:14.930
First of all, thank you both so much for giving your time today.
首先，非常感谢你们今天抽出宝贵的时间。

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:19.970
Josh, I'd love you to start by introducing us to our shared guest today.
乔什，我希望你首先向我们介绍今天的共同嘉宾。

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:21.930
Right now?
现在？

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:22.970
Yes, please.
是的，请。

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.649
All right, all right.
好吧，好吧。

00:05:24.649 --> 00:05:37.730
Well, I am thrilled to be sharing the airwaves with my dear friend, Deb Lapidus, who I met back in the 90s at NYU at the Graduate Acting Program.
嗯，我很高兴能与我亲爱的朋友黛布·拉皮德斯 (Deb Lapidus) 分享广播节目，我是在 90 年代在纽约大学研究生表演项目中认识的。

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:41.730
I was there 1996 to 1999.
1996年到1999年我在那里。

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:46.569
My life has been very blessed with mentors, both in and out of the art space, I'll just say that.
我的生活非常幸运，有艺术界内外的导师，我只想说。

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:50.250
So I had a lot of people I could have chosen from.
所以我有很多人可以选择。

00:05:50.250 --> 00:06:08.009
I think the reason I chose Deb is because in addition to Deb being so influential in my New York pre-professional training, I've just maintained a friendship with her, and she's a friend, and I feel like any opportunity to just grab some time with her feels like a treat.
我想我选择黛布的原因除了黛布对我在纽约的职业前培训有如此大的影响之外，我还和她保持着友谊，她是我的朋友，我觉得任何能和她在一起的机会都像是一种享受。

00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:10.610
Not that my other mentors were ogres or anything.
并不是说我的其他导师是食人魔或其他什么东西。

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.170
I just really love Deb.
我真的很爱黛布。

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:14.329
I'm looking at her now.
我现在正在看着她。

00:06:14.329 --> 00:06:15.329
I haven't seen her for a while.
我有一段时间没见到她了。

00:06:15.329 --> 00:06:19.810
I also should also say that Deb is the Paul Rudd of music teachers.
我还应该说黛布是音乐老师中的保罗·路德。

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:20.970
She does not age.
她不老。

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:24.529
She looks exactly the same, and she did 25 years ago.
她看起来和25年前一模一样。

00:06:24.529 --> 00:06:26.129
I'm not kidding.
我不是在开玩笑。

00:06:26.129 --> 00:06:39.269
You know, there are certain things I was thinking about that have lingered from what Deb taught me, and I will say this, she was one of the great acting teachers at NYU, even though she is a singing teacher.
你知道，有些事情我一直在思考，黛布教给我的东西一直萦绕在我的脑海里，我要说的是，她是纽约大学最伟大的表演老师之一，尽管她是一名歌唱老师。

00:06:39.269 --> 00:06:42.951
So please welcome my dear friend and teacher, Deb Lapidus.
欢迎我亲爱的朋友和老师 Deb Lapidus。

00:06:42.951 --> 00:06:43.451
Hi.
你好。

00:06:43.451 --> 00:06:43.959
Hi, Deb.
嗨，黛布。

00:06:43.959 --> 00:06:45.356
It's great to see you.
很高兴见到你。

00:06:45.356 --> 00:06:46.879
I'm so happy to see you.
我很高兴见到你。

00:06:47.529 --> 00:06:48.805
Thanks for being here.
感谢您来到这里。

00:06:48.805 --> 00:06:51.529
It brings a smile to my face, is all I can say.
我只能说，它给我带来了微笑。

00:06:51.529 --> 00:06:52.529
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:52.529 --> 00:06:53.529
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:53.529 --> 00:07:05.250
So a fun way to start the conversation has been for each of you to talk about or tell the story of the first time you remember working together.
因此，开始对话的一个有趣的方式是让你们每个人谈论或讲述你们第一次一起工作的故事。

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:12.850
And it's totally okay if it's not the same story, actually, probably better if it's not the same story.
如果不是同一个故事也完全没问题，实际上，如果不是同一个故事可能会更好。

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:18.449
But that has been a really nice way for folks to be able to start being in conversation with one another.
但这对于人们能够开始相互交谈来说是一种非常好的方式。

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:19.449
You go, Josh.
你走吧，乔什。

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:20.449
What do you think, Deb?
你觉得怎么样，黛布？

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:21.059
All right.
好的。

00:07:21.059 --> 00:07:22.767
I grew up in Columbus, Ohio.
我在俄亥俄州哥伦布长大。

00:07:22.767 --> 00:07:28.440
I went to school in Ohio at Kenyon College, a little small school in a hilltop in rural Ohio.
我在俄亥俄州的凯尼恩学院上学，这是一所位于俄亥俄州乡村山顶的小学校。

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:38.687
So my getting accepted to NYU at my senior year of college and coming to New York when I had just turned 22 was a pretty profound, somewhat vertigo inducing experience.
因此，我在大学四年级时被纽约大学录取，并在我刚满 22 岁的时候来到纽约，这是一次相当深刻、有点令人眩晕的经历。

00:07:38.687 --> 00:07:44.543
At that point, I mean, I don't know where it stands now, but NYU was a very hot school to go to.
那时，我的意思是，我不知道它现在处于什么位置，但纽约大学是一所非常热门的学校。

00:07:44.543 --> 00:07:46.312
And I was incredibly nervous.
我非常紧张。

00:07:46.312 --> 00:07:54.302
I mean, I had a kind sophisticated idea of what it meant to be an actor, but a more provincial kind of sense of myself as a person.
我的意思是，我对作为一名演员意味着什么有一种复杂的想法，但对自己作为一个人的感觉却更为狭隘。

00:07:54.302 --> 00:07:58.633
And I was learning the subways and learning how to be in New York City.
我正在学习地铁并学习如何生活在纽约市。

00:07:58.633 --> 00:08:00.768
I mean, it was really overwhelming.
我的意思是，这真的是压倒性的。

00:08:00.768 --> 00:08:08.027
So I think I thought of New York and the theater and the arts as this velvet rope that I was dying to get asked behind.
所以我想我把纽约、剧院和艺术想象成一根天鹅绒绳子，我渴望被人问到背后的问题。

00:08:08.027 --> 00:08:13.943
But at the same time, it felt like this mystical land of it was like a species different than me.
但同时，又感觉这片神秘的土地就像是一个与我不同的物种。

00:08:13.943 --> 00:08:18.396
And I remember meeting Deb and there was something so familiar about her.
我记得见到黛布时，她身上有一种很熟悉的感觉。

00:08:18.396 --> 00:08:23.703
I just recognized her as, I mean, she felt like she could have been a relative of mine.
我只是认出了她，我的意思是，她觉得她可能是我的亲戚。

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:26.570
She probably is, if we trace it back, you know.
如果我们追溯一下，你知道，她可能是。

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:29.869
Same stettle, we always say like family.
一样的，我们总是说像家人一样。

00:08:29.869 --> 00:08:43.180
So there was something in her ability to bridge Naive, Ohio, Jewish boy confused in New York for the first time me with coming into this different sense of myself as an artist who actually had a right to be there.
因此，她有能力帮助我，俄亥俄州的一个犹太男孩在纽约感到困惑，这是我第一次对自己作为一名真正有权在那里的艺术家产生不同的认识。

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:49.929
One of the great things about Deb is she's one of the funniest people I know and her humor was so disarming.
黛布最伟大的事情之一就是她是我认识的最有趣的人之一，她的幽默是如此令人放松。

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:55.166
I remember being in her class and it didn't feel like I needed to decode anything.
我记得在她的课上，感觉我不需要解码任何东西。

00:08:55.166 --> 00:08:57.721
It felt like, oh, I know how to do this.
感觉就像，哦，我知道该怎么做。

00:08:57.721 --> 00:09:02.511
We sing and we try to communicate the depth and text and meaning of a song.
我们唱歌并试图传达歌曲的深度、文本和含义。

00:09:02.511 --> 00:09:07.300
There was just something clear and welcoming about her style and her class.
她的风格和她的班级有一些清晰和热情的东西。

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.941
I felt at home and I felt like I had a right to be there.
我有宾至如归的感觉，我觉得我有权利呆在那里。

00:09:10.941 --> 00:09:12.409
What do you think, Deb?
你觉得怎么样，黛布？

00:09:12.409 --> 00:09:28.300
Well, you know, it's interesting to hear you speak about it because you might have been nervous, but you always felt like you had savvy, with you're not describing yourself as you're describing yourself as provincial, but I never actually found you that way.
嗯，你知道，听到你谈论这件事很有趣，因为你可能很紧张，但你总是觉得自己很有悟性，你并没有像描述自己那样乡下人来描述自己，但我实际上从未发现你是这样的。

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:40.864
I mean, one of the things is that when I when I teach it and while you as I teach actors to sing, and there's a kind of a big range of what the ability level in the room is and also the appetite to do it.
我的意思是，其中一件事是，当我教演员唱歌时，当你教演员唱歌时，房间里的能力水平以及这样做的兴趣都有很大的范围。

00:09:40.864 --> 00:09:41.850
It just depends.
这只是取决于。

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:51.370
With Josh, the way he felt about me is how I felt about Josh, just felt like we knew each other already, just something, just felt very familiar and comfortable about Josh.
对于乔希，他对我的感觉就是我对乔希的感觉，只是感觉我们已经认识对方了，只是对乔希感到非常熟悉和舒服。

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:53.937
And also the other thing is that he was witty.
还有一点就是他很机智。

00:09:53.937 --> 00:09:57.901
And I know he mentioned that I'm funny, which I am, but he's funny too.
我知道他提到我很有趣，确实如此，但他也很有趣。

00:09:57.901 --> 00:10:00.413
And I don't think that many people are funny.
而且我不认为很多人是有趣的。

00:10:00.413 --> 00:10:02.701
I think very few people are really funny.
我认为真正有趣的人很少。

00:10:02.701 --> 00:10:09.121
And it's sometimes hard, Josh, I'm sure you must feel this way to keep the ball in the air, to amuse everyone else.
有时这很困难，乔什，我相信你一定有这种感觉，才能让球保持在空中，让其他人开心。

00:10:09.121 --> 00:10:13.531
So that when you get someone who can actually amuse you, it's pretty darn good.
所以，当你遇到一个真正能让你开心的人时，那就太好了。

00:10:13.531 --> 00:10:16.490
But the other thing about Josh is that he was sparky.
但乔什的另一件事是他很有活力。

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.142
He had a kind of spark to him and energy.
他身上有一种火花和能量。

00:10:19.142 --> 00:10:25.028
And even though you might have been nervous, you didn't wallow in a kind of act or despair.
即使你可能很紧张，但你并没有陷入某种行为或绝望之中。

00:10:25.028 --> 00:10:26.451
I don't remember that.
我不记得了。

00:10:26.451 --> 00:10:42.753
Maybe you remember that differently, but that kind of act or despair of, oh my God, I'm so bad and I used to be able to act and I can't act anymore and I used to be able to sing and I can't sing anymore, that kind of energy I did not remember from you.
也许你的记忆有所不同，但那种行为或绝望，天哪，我太糟糕了，我曾经能够表演，但我不能再表演了，我曾经能够唱歌，但我不能再唱歌了，那种能量我不记得你了。

00:10:42.753 --> 00:10:46.570
I just remember you having an appetite to kind of be there.
我只记得你有兴趣去那里。

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:53.289
And in terms of what you said about code switching, I always felt you were absolutely yourself.
至于你所说的语码转换，我一直觉得你绝对是你自己。

00:10:53.289 --> 00:10:54.517
You know, it's funny.
你知道，这很有趣。

00:10:54.517 --> 00:11:00.302
I, our memory of our internal state is often at odds with what we projected, especially first year.
我，我们对内部状态的记忆常常与我们的预测不一致，尤其是第一年。

00:11:00.302 --> 00:11:09.769
And this is a problem with first year actors at a drama program is that every time you get up to perform or sing a song, you continually think you're auditioning.
戏剧节目中一年级演员的一个问题是，每次你站起来表演或唱歌时，你都会不断地认为你正在试镜。

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:21.889
And that every moment in drama school is a referendum on not just your ability to be an actor in the professional context, but a referendum on your soul and your worth as a human being.
在戏剧学校的每一刻，不仅是对你作为专业演员的能力的一次公投，也是对你的灵魂和作为一个人的价值的公投。

00:11:21.889 --> 00:11:24.330
And it can be very stressful.
这可能会带来很大的压力。

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.929
I didn't feel that as much in your class.
我在你的课上没有那么强烈的感觉。

00:11:26.929 --> 00:11:35.704
I felt like there was some reprieve from the, there was a very healthy permission to fail, thought in your class, which I rather liked.
我觉得你们的课堂上有一种非常健康的失败许可，我很喜欢这一点。

00:11:35.704 --> 00:11:41.229
I felt like you didn't care if someone was a Broadway level singer or a total novice.
我觉得你并不关心某人是百老汇级别的歌手还是完全的新手。

00:11:41.229 --> 00:11:45.129
I felt like you really dealt with people on their own terms.
我觉得你真的按照自己的方式与人打交道。

00:11:45.769 --> 00:11:53.048
I wanted so desperately to be an actor, but I also wanted to do it with as little stress to my nervous system as I could.
我非常想成为一名演员，但我也想在尽可能不给我的神经系统造成压力的情况下做到这一点。

00:11:53.048 --> 00:12:01.769
I had my spell second year where I was really in a lot of turmoil, but Mostly, I treated drama school like going to law school or medical school.
第二年我确实陷入了很多混乱，但大多数情况下，我对待戏剧学校就像去法学院或医学院一样。

00:12:01.769 --> 00:12:05.970
Like I was trying to acquire the skills that would allow me to have a career.
就像我试图获得能让我从事职业的技能一样。

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:11.090
And it was hard because I think sometimes in drama schools, the squeaky wheel gets the grease.
这很难，因为我认为有时在戏剧学校，吱吱作响的轮子会得到润滑。

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:16.330
Like the people who are a little more dramatic seem to get a little more attention.
就像那些更具戏剧性的人似乎会得到更多的关注。

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.250
Still true to this day.
直到今天仍然如此。

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:26.230
Yeah, you're also dealing with these very kind of unformed, often very talented and dysfunctional lumps of clay.
是的，你还要面对这些未成型的、通常非常有才华但功能失调的粘土块。

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:30.450
you probably see an astonishing transformation of people from their first year to the third year.
你可能会看到人们从第一年到第三年发生了惊人的转变。

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.769
Yeah, definitely.
是的，绝对是。

00:12:31.769 --> 00:12:43.250
And also seeing people sometimes right after they get out from graduating to then maybe being in a show on stage and actually remember having this experience.
有时也会看到人们刚毕业就在舞台上表演，并且确实记得有过这种经历。

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:55.649
And I tell the students this sometimes because as a teacher, a lot of times it feels like you're kind of making a sculpture and you're right on top of it and you're kind of sculpting the fingernail or sculpting the vein and the neck or whatever.
有时我告诉学生这一点，因为作为一名老师，很多时候感觉就像你在制作一个雕塑，而你就在它的上面，你在雕刻指甲或雕刻静脉和颈部或其他什么。

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:58.289
But usually you're not stepping back.
但通常你不会后退。

00:12:58.289 --> 00:13:06.769
Sometimes I think we get so detail oriented where you start to see all the things you wanna change for want of a better word.
有时我认为我们变得如此注重细节，你开始看到所有你想要改变的事情，因为缺乏更好的词。

00:13:06.769 --> 00:13:20.529
And then sometimes when you step back and you look at the whole person, you're kind of bold over and get a totally different kind of take on the person and a kind of different hit on who they are and what they're able to do.
有时，当你退后一步，审视整个人时，你会变得大胆起来，对这个人有一种完全不同的看法，对他们是谁以及他们能够做什么有一种不同的看法。

00:13:20.529 --> 00:13:29.389
And I've literally been almost blinded sometimes by someone's sheer talent that I've seen on the stage that I worked with.
有时，我在与我合作的舞台上看到的某人的纯粹才华有时几乎让我蒙蔽了双眼。

00:13:29.389 --> 00:13:31.669
I experienced them differently.
我对他们的经历有所不同。

00:13:31.669 --> 00:13:37.149
You mean like if you see them 10 years out, even less sometimes than the person.
你的意思是，如果你十年后再见到他们，有时甚至比那个人还要短。

00:13:37.149 --> 00:13:42.470
Because I'm also watching them not so much as teacher, but as audience.
因为我也不是以老师的身份观看他们，而是以观众的身份观看他们。

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:50.500
And that's a different kind of way of taking somebody in and also seeing the whole in a different kind of way.
这是一种不同的方式，让人们了解并以不同的方式看待整体。

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:51.580
Does that make any sense?
这有什么意义吗？

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:52.980
Yeah, totally.
是的，完全可以。

00:13:52.980 --> 00:14:05.700
So Josh used this great phrase, healthy permission to fail, which he described as a feature of your class and one of the things that he really valued about you as an educator.
因此，乔什使用了这个伟大的短语，“健康地允许失败”，他将其描述为你们班级的一个特点，也是他作为一名教育者真正看重的事情之一。

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:15.700
So I was curious first if that resonated with you as something that you actively think about and how you make that happen in your classroom or in your learning space.
所以我首先很好奇这是否会引起你的共鸣，作为你积极思考的事情，以及你如何在课堂或学习空间中实现这一点。

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:22.980
Well, I mean, I think the idea of success versus failure is something that I'm not amazingly interested in.
嗯，我的意思是，我认为我对成功与失败的想法并不是很感兴趣。

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:30.379
So by that, I mean, I think that students tend to think they have to be successful or they have to succeed.
因此，我的意思是，我认为学生倾向于认为他们必须成功，或者他们必须成功。

00:14:30.379 --> 00:14:35.340
And I'm just talking about like in a class, on a song, on a scene, whatever.
我只是说在课堂上，在歌曲中，在场景中，等等。

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.220
To me, that's not important.
对我来说，这并不重要。

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:41.379
What's important to me is doing the task at hand, doing the work.
对我来说重要的是完成手头的任务，做好工作。

00:14:41.379 --> 00:14:47.779
And the work is a combination as Ron Van Lu said of technical things and also things from the heart.
正如 Ron Van Lu 所说，这项工作是技术性的东西和发自内心的东西的结合。

00:14:47.779 --> 00:14:51.720
There's nothing that interests me less than just purely technical singing.
没有什么比纯粹的技术歌唱更让我感兴趣的了。

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:53.639
I like to feel there's a person at home.
我喜欢家里有一个人的感觉。

00:14:53.639 --> 00:14:55.000
I wanna know who the person is.
我想知道这个人是谁。

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:08.759
I wanna feel like I know the person more through their work, not because they're telling me who they are in a dinner conversation, but because through their work and through the text, whoever, whatever the writer is, I'm learning more about them.
我想感觉我通过他们的工作更多地了解这个人，不是因为他们在晚餐谈话中告诉我他们是谁，而是因为通过他们的工作和文本，无论是谁，无论作者是谁，我都在更多地了解他们。

00:15:08.759 --> 00:15:12.879
That was something Josh always was good at, by the way.
顺便说一句，这是乔什一直擅长的事情。

00:15:12.879 --> 00:15:22.919
So by permission to fail, to me that means to do the work at hand and not necessarily worry if you're getting a quote unquote right.
因此，对我来说，允许失败意味着完成手头的工作，而不必担心是否正确引用。

00:15:22.919 --> 00:15:29.759
And I think that the idea of getting it right is the thing that can really limit people as they're trying to do their work.
我认为把事情做好的想法确实会限制人们努力做好自己的工作。

00:15:29.759 --> 00:15:36.279
Because when you try to get it right, you're actually working towards the end and not actually in where you are.
因为当你试图把事情做好时，你实际上是在朝着终点努力，而不是在你现在的位置上。

00:15:36.279 --> 00:15:38.200
You're thinking about is this correct?
你在想这是正确的吗？

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:39.759
And you're second guessing yourself.
你又开始怀疑自己了。

00:15:39.759 --> 00:15:45.279
And the fact of the matter is the word right or correct is different for everybody.
事实上，正确或正确这个词对于每个人来说都是不同的。

00:15:45.279 --> 00:15:49.679
I remember someone in your class, Josh, who had a lot of pitch problems.
我记得你们班上有一个人，乔什，有很多音调问题。

00:15:49.679 --> 00:15:54.159
And for that person, a tremendous success would be just singing in two.
对于那个人来说，一唱两唱就已经是巨大的成功了。

00:15:54.159 --> 00:15:57.159
And for you, that would not be a big success.
对你来说，这不会是一个巨大的成功。

00:15:57.159 --> 00:16:00.960
If we wanna use success and failure as two things.
如果我们想将成功和失败视为两件事。

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:06.159
So for me, the only way to really fail is to not try in a kind of way.
所以对我来说，真正失败的唯一方法就是不以某种方式尝试。

00:16:06.159 --> 00:16:20.320
And there are people that are, they're adept, they can do things, but they don't necessarily put their heart into those things, or they've just been always good at something and they can give kind of a very small amount and still be quote unquote good.
有些人很擅长，他们可以做事，但他们不一定全心投入这些事情，或者他们只是一直擅长某件事，他们可以付出很少的金额，但仍然是好的。

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.000
To me, that's a failure.
对我来说，这是一次失败。

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:33.200
Even though somebody else might perceive that person as good or talented, I just find them not actually putting all theirself into whatever the task at hand is.
尽管其他人可能认为这个人很好或有才华，但我只是发现他们并没有真正全身心投入到手头的任务中。

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:35.399
And to me, that is how you fail.
对我来说，这就是你失败的原因。

00:16:35.399 --> 00:16:42.000
Trying to do something that you don't do very well is one of the most courageous things you can possibly do.
尝试做一些你不太擅长的事情是你能做的最勇敢的事情之一。

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:53.860
And because I teach singing, which has a lot of baggage for a lot of people, and there's a lot of people who have been told, you don't sing, you're bad, you're not as good as your sister, you're not as good as this person.
因为我教唱歌，这对很多人来说有很多包袱，很多人被告知，你不唱歌，你很糟糕，你不如你姐姐，你不如这个人。

00:16:53.860 --> 00:17:00.059
And then in the room itself, people also can feel, wow, well, Josh is really good, but so-and-so is not so good.
然后在房间本身，人们也能感觉到，哇，嗯，乔什真的很好，但是某某不太好。

00:17:00.059 --> 00:17:02.500
And also, there's nowhere to hide when you sing.
而且，当你唱歌时，无处可躲。

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:18.740
So if you're singing, that's coming from you in a way that feels so personal, and whatever is going on, Sometimes in the material what the song is talking about or sometimes in your life, it's very hard to finesse that away from something that's true.
所以，如果你在唱歌，那是你以一种感觉如此个人化的方式发出的，无论发生什么，有时在歌曲所谈论的材料中，有时在你的生活中，很难巧妙地将其与真实的东西分开。

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:21.779
You do see people with a lot of raw truth.
你确实看到人们有很多原始的真相。

00:17:21.779 --> 00:17:27.180
And to me, it's the people that just don't make the attempt to do something incredibly hard for them.
对我来说，人们只是没有尝试为他们做一些极其困难的事情。

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:39.859
And I think in a way, and Josh, you could speak to this, doing something really hard for you, be it singing or dancing or acting in the Shakespeare play or whatever, is something I think as an actor you have to do?
我认为在某种程度上，乔什，你可以说，做一些对你来说非常困难的事情，无论是唱歌、跳舞还是在莎士比亚戏剧中表演或其他什么，我认为作为一名演员你必须做的事情吗？

00:17:39.859 --> 00:17:48.500
Yeah, I mean, NYU, in some ways, is just a daily exposure to your limits and your tolerance for shame.
是的，我的意思是，纽约大学，在某些方面，只是每天暴露你的极限和你对羞耻的容忍度。

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:56.980
Yeah, I remember just being humiliated quite regularly at NYU, and I think that's part of it.
是的，我记得在纽约大学经常受到羞辱，我认为这就是其中的一部分。

00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:11.619
If you read the Mike Nichols biography that Mark Harris just wrote, Jack Nicholson, before big scenes, would do this crazy, ridiculous dance and make all these ridiculous noises, he said there, nothing I do will be as humiliating as what I just did.
如果你读过马克·哈里斯刚刚写的迈克·尼科尔斯传记，杰克·尼科尔森在大场面之前，会跳这种疯狂、可笑的舞蹈，发出所有这些可笑的声音，他在那里说，我所做的任何事情都不会像我刚才所做的那样令人羞辱。

00:18:11.619 --> 00:18:13.971
You know, let's get that out of the way.
你知道，让我们解决这个问题。

00:18:13.971 --> 00:18:16.323
And so I feel like that was part of NYU.
所以我觉得那是纽约大学的一部分。

00:18:16.323 --> 00:18:23.143
NYU, in a lot of ways, was much more rigorous, embarrassing, and difficult than most of what I faced professionally.
从很多方面来说，纽约大学比我在职业生涯中遇到的大多数情况都要严格、尴尬和困难得多。

00:18:23.143 --> 00:18:35.019
I mean, professionally, the getting a job or not getting a job and auditioning, all that can be quite treacherous, but the actual work, you get cast in roles you're quite appropriate for professionally.
我的意思是，从专业角度来说，找工作或不找到工作以及试镜，所有这些都可能是相当危险的，但在实际工作中，你会扮演在专业上非常适合的角色。

00:18:35.019 --> 00:18:43.303
At NYU, you get cast off in a role as you're very inappropriate for that you would never get cast in professionally and you have to stretch yourself.
在纽约大学，你会被淘汰，因为你非常不适合这个角色，因为你永远不会成为专业人士，你必须拓展自己。

00:18:43.303 --> 00:18:57.701
But I did want to speak to one thing you were talking about when I said that you were one of the best acting teachers at NYU, my memory of your class, you would, you know, relax your jaw and stretch out your tongue, but you're not an overly technical teacher.
但我确实想谈谈你所说的一件事，当我说你是纽约大学最好的表演老师之一时，我对你的课的记忆，你会，你知道，放松你的下巴，伸出你的舌头，但你不是一个过于技术性的老师。

00:18:57.701 --> 00:19:01.425
And most of how you coach a student through a song is text you all.
大多数指导学生唱歌曲的方式都是发短信给大家。

00:19:01.425 --> 00:19:03.927
That you say, well, what are you saying here?
你说，好吧，你在这里说什么？

00:19:03.927 --> 00:19:05.150
And what does it mean?
这意味着什么？

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:07.318
And what are you trying to communicate?
你想传达什么？

00:19:07.318 --> 00:19:12.210
You would break it down like it was a Shakespeare monologue or any other kind of speech.
你会像莎士比亚独白或任何其他类型的演讲一样分解它。

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:16.380
What I found was for both me and I would observe it over and over and over.
我发现的东西对我来说是适合的，我会一遍又一遍地观察它。

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:20.601
If someone was hooked up to the meaning, their breath was in the right place.
如果有人明白其中的含义，那么他们的呼吸就在正确的位置。

00:19:20.601 --> 00:19:21.862
They had enough breath.
他们的呼吸已经足够了。

00:19:21.862 --> 00:19:25.206
They would get to the end of the line that they were singing.
他们会唱到他们所唱的那句歌词的末尾。

00:19:25.206 --> 00:19:58.537
So I found that you were much less interested in breath control and pitch than you were in what are you trying to communicate why are you singing this song what are you doing up there something that you said I think about more than almost anything I learned in NYU and I think about it a lot as a writer actually you said play to the smartest people in the room always play to the smartest people in the room and that has been so meaningful to me to not sand the edges off my stuff the people who this is meant who are going to get it or going to get it and maybe someone else to get it on a different level.
所以我发现你对呼吸控制和音高的兴趣远不如你想表达的东西为什么你唱这首歌你在那里做什么你说的我想的比我在纽约大学学到的几乎所有东西都多，我作为一个作家思考了很多实际上你说为房间里最聪明的人演奏总是为房间里最聪明的人演奏，这对我来说非常有意义，不要磨掉我的东西的边缘，这意味着那些将要得到它或将要得到它的人它也许还有其他人可以将其提升到不同的水平。

00:19:58.537 --> 00:20:01.388
But that really echoes in my ear that you said that.
但我的耳边确实回响着你所说的话。

00:20:01.388 --> 00:20:04.019
So I just wanted to thank you publicly for that.
所以我只是想公开感谢你。

00:20:04.019 --> 00:20:05.177
Oh, well, thank you.
哦，好的，谢谢你。

00:20:05.177 --> 00:20:06.161
I still say that.
我仍然这么说。

00:20:06.161 --> 00:20:14.903
And it's I think it's really important to play the smartest people because sometimes people on stage or whatever they feel they have to explain things.
我认为扮演最聪明的人非常重要，因为有时舞台上的人或他们认为必须解释事情的人。

00:20:14.903 --> 00:20:16.524
And maybe you don't have to.
也许你不必这样做。

00:20:16.524 --> 00:20:19.940
Maybe you maybe someone will understand what you're saying.
也许你也许有人会明白你在说什么。

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:28.099
Well, it's also an invitation to the audience, you know, like Billy Wilder said this great that if you let the audience put two and two together, they'll love you forever.
嗯，这也是对观众的邀请，你知道，就像比利·怀尔德说的那样，如果你让观众把两个和两个放在一起，他们会永远爱你。

00:20:28.099 --> 00:20:29.339
Right, exactly, right.
对，对，对。

00:20:29.339 --> 00:20:31.220
You know, let them do their work, right?
你知道，让他们做他们的工作，对吧？

00:20:31.220 --> 00:20:32.579
Right, that's right.
没错，就是这样。

00:20:32.579 --> 00:20:34.579
But thank you, Josh, I appreciate that.
但谢谢你，乔什，我很感激。

00:20:34.579 --> 00:20:35.539
And I still believe it.
我仍然相信这一点。

00:20:35.539 --> 00:20:37.220
And this is why I love actors.
这就是我喜欢演员的原因。

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:45.140
Working with people that have an ability to tell a story, to transform, to become a character.
与有能力讲述故事、转变、成为角色的人一起工作。

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:48.700
When you have that and a voice, to me, that's everything.
当你拥有这样的能力和声音时，对我来说，这就是一切。

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:54.502
There's nothing more boring to me than just watching someone basically make the music of the song.
对我来说，没有什么比看着某人创作这首歌的音乐更无聊的了。

00:20:54.502 --> 00:20:59.180
I mean, you know, I'll take Ella if it's Gerald, but you know, very few others.
我的意思是，你知道，如果是杰拉德，我会选择艾拉，但你知道，很少有其他人。

00:20:59.180 --> 00:20:59.680
Yeah.
是的。

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:05.774
I always felt like when you were moved by something, you were very moved by it.
我总觉得，当你被某件事感动时，你就会被它深深感动。

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:15.822
You let yourself be affected by what was happening in the room, whether it was insane, ridiculous, moving, heartbreaking, frustrating.
你让自己受到房间里发生的事情的影响，无论它是疯狂的、荒谬的、感人的、心碎的、令人沮丧的。

00:21:15.822 --> 00:21:18.779
You really felt in the sandbox with us.
你真的感觉和我们一起在沙箱里。

00:21:18.779 --> 00:21:32.619
When I started as a teacher, I think I used to feel that it was not appropriate to show too much emotion, that I should be a little bit more removed, or maybe I felt a little embarrassed if I would really start to cry in some way.
当我开始当老师的时候，我想我曾经觉得表现出太多的情感是不合适的，我应该远离一点，或者如果我真的开始以某种方式哭泣，也许我会感到有点尴尬。

00:21:32.619 --> 00:21:37.160
And then there was a point at which I just realized that that was not useful.
然后到了某个时刻我才意识到这没有用。

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:44.839
And I decided to just let myself go, and if it felt uncomfortable to me, or if I felt I was being judged by others, so be it.
我决定放手一搏，如果我觉得不舒服，或者我觉得别人在评判我，那就这样吧。

00:21:44.839 --> 00:21:54.119
But I do remember that is something that was not always true for me as a teacher in terms of allowing my responses to be quite as apparent.
但我确实记得，作为一名教师，我的反应并不总是如此明显。

00:21:54.119 --> 00:22:10.759
Because actors are asked to be so vulnerable and to allow things to happen to them and to be seen, I feel that it's not really fair for a teacher to hold back on the things they're asking for actors to do if there's going to be a really creative exchange in the room.
因为演员被要求表现得如此脆弱，并允许事情发生在他们身上并被看到，所以我觉得如果房间里要进行真正有创意的交流，老师就阻止他们要求演员做的事情是不公平的。

00:22:10.759 --> 00:22:16.839
You have to put yourself out there as a teacher in a way as much as the students put themselves out there.
作为一名教师，你必须像学生一样展现自己。

00:22:16.839 --> 00:22:19.039
And they have to know it's safe and that you're human.
他们必须知道这是安全的并且你是人类。

00:22:19.039 --> 00:22:35.119
It's been interesting because sometimes lately when I've expressed myself with great conviction in a way that it doesn't feel very sitting back but actually really participatory, I can feel that they like it because they know that there's a heart beating underneath all of that.
这很有趣，因为最近有时当我以一种非常有信念的方式表达自己时，感觉不是很坐立不安，而是实际上真正参与，我能感觉到他们喜欢它，因为他们知道这一切的背后有一颗跳动的心。

00:22:35.119 --> 00:22:42.559
And it's important to know that I can get angry too or I don't have to stay very removed to actually be a good teacher.
重要的是要知道我也会生气，或者我不必保持非常远离才能真正成为一名好老师。

00:22:42.559 --> 00:23:06.400
Josh, when I was going to interrupt you before, I'm glad I didn't because Deb said almost literally word for word the thing that I was going to say, which is, you know, a huge message of the book that I wrote is that scaffolding students to take risks is the single most important component to being a successful classroom teacher.
乔什，当我之前打算打断你的时候，我很高兴我没有打断你，因为黛布几乎逐字逐句地说出了我要说的话，你知道，我写的书中传达的一个重要信息是，培养学生承担风险是成为一名成功的课堂教师最重要的组成部分。

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:15.579
So risk taking is the fundamental move in how you learn something new regardless of the discipline.
因此，无论学科如何，冒险是学习新东西的基本举措。

00:23:15.579 --> 00:23:41.869
And you know, for me the arts for obvious reasons that we've discussed is the prototypical space where that happens, but the same thing is true in the math classroom and the science classroom that to stretch yourself as a learner and as a child to try and be wrong as a pathway to to building more robust understanding is the single most important thing in the classroom.
你知道，对我来说，出于我们已经讨论过的显而易见的原因，艺术是发生这种情况的典型空间，但在数学课堂和科学课堂上也是如此，作为一个学习者和一个孩子，不断尝试和犯错，作为建立更强大理解的途径是课堂上最重要的事情。

00:23:41.869 --> 00:23:53.990
Once again, we're gonna take a moment to step away from our conversation so that Erika can relate some of what Josh and Deb were saying here to the kinds of things that she was thinking about that made her write her book, How the Arts Can Save Education.
我们将再次花点时间离开我们的谈话，以便埃里卡能够将乔什和黛布在这里所说的一些内容与她正在思考的各种事情联系起来，这些事情促使她写下了她的书《艺术如何拯救教育》。

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:57.230
Or on the office, we call this Erika saying smart stuff.
或者在办公室，我们称埃里卡为“聪明人”。

00:23:57.230 --> 00:24:00.029
It starts with a question from me, and here we go.
它从我的一个问题开始，我们开始吧。

00:24:00.029 --> 00:24:02.589
Two things that jump out, I think, that are obviously related.
我认为有两件事明显相关。

00:24:02.589 --> 00:24:12.849
They talk a lot about risk taking, which you definitely do if you don't consider yourself a singer and you stand out to stand up in class and sing in front of the class, I can't even imagine the horrors.
他们谈论了很多关于冒险的话题，如果你不认为自己是一名歌手，但你站起来在课堂上在全班同学面前唱歌，你肯定会这么做，我什至无法想象那是多么恐怖。

00:24:12.849 --> 00:24:17.849
And also about what you in your book called Reclaiming Failure.
还有你在书中所说的“回收失败”。

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:23.170
So the idea that failure isn't necessarily actually failure.
因此，失败的想法并不一定是真正的失败。

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:24.009
Talk to us.
与我们交谈。

00:24:24.009 --> 00:24:38.470
So one of the big learnings I've had from spending a lot of time in schools is we frame the discussion about learning in terms of the acquisition of stuff.
因此，我在学校度过的大量时间学到的一大教训是，我们从获取东西的角度来讨论学习。

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:39.829
Did you get this?
你明白了吗？

00:24:39.829 --> 00:24:44.230
Can you demonstrate on a test that you know the information?
您能在测试中证明您了解这些信息吗？

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:49.069
Can you do a thing now that you couldn't do before?
你现在能做一些以前做不到的事情吗？

00:24:49.069 --> 00:24:52.710
And that frames failure as a lack of learning.
这将失败归结为缺乏学习。

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:54.829
And we apply that lack of learning.
我们利用了缺乏学习的情况。

00:24:54.829 --> 00:24:56.470
We apply it to students, obviously.
显然，我们将其应用于学生。

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:58.829
You failed a course, you failed a test.
你没通过课程，你没通过考试。

00:24:58.829 --> 00:25:01.549
We apply it to the discourse of schools, right?
我们将其应用到学校的话语中，对吧？

00:25:01.549 --> 00:25:10.630
Many of our listeners may have heard the phrase failing schools, where there's some checklist of things that schools do or don't do.
我们的许多听众可能听说过“失败的学校”这个词，其中有一些学校做或不做的事情的清单。

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:18.150
In my experience, failure is actually better characterized as a part of the learning process.
根据我的经验，失败实际上最好被描述为学习过程的一部分。

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:23.150
And we should expect failure as part of the process.
我们应该预见到失败是这个过程的一部分。

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:42.289
But because we are so trained to think of failure as a thing to be avoided at all costs, as designers of learning experiences, which many of these mentors, including Deb are, we have to set up the conditions for people to feel like failure is part of learning.
但因为我们受过如此训练，认为失败是不惜一切代价避免的事情，作为学习体验的设计者，包括黛布在内的许多导师就是这样，我们必须创造条件，让人们觉得失败是学习的一部分。

00:25:42.289 --> 00:26:12.750
That requires, as you said, Alec, risk-taking, to be able to take risks so that we know that if something doesn't go the way we expected, there's as much juicy goodness in the thing that we don't expect as there is in being able to successfully quote unquote, do the thing that we think gives us the good grade, gives us the right answer, gives us the quality metric on the school evaluation system.
正如你所说，亚历克，这需要冒险，能够承担风险，这样我们就知道，如果事情没有按照我们预期的方式发展，那么我们没有预料到的事情与能够成功引用不引用的事情一样多汁的好处，做我们认为会给我们带来好成绩的事情，给我们正确的答案，给我们学校评估系统的质量指标。

00:26:13.049 --> 00:26:26.130
And like many of these other topics that we have discussed and we will discuss, the arts brings the idea into sharp relief, but that applies to everything we do in schools.
就像我们已经讨论过和将要讨论的许多其他主题一样，艺术使这个想法更加突出，但这适用于我们在学校所做的一切。

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:36.160
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
大家好，我是 Alex Goldman，Hyper-Fixed 播客的主持人。

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:39.839
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并尝试为他们解决。

00:26:39.839 --> 00:26:42.839
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
诸如此类的问题，我已经 30 岁了，我害怕在纽约开车。

00:26:42.839 --> 00:26:46.519
Or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者为什么我倒车时无法调节汽车音响的音量？

00:26:46.519 --> 00:26:49.079
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们还解决非汽车相关的问题。

00:26:49.079 --> 00:26:55.230
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果您遇到问题，我们不仅会修复它，还会首先暴露导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:57.750
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这是来自 Radiotopia 的 Hyper-Fixed 播客。

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:12.059
wherever you find podcasts or at hyperfixedpod.com.
无论您在哪里找到播客或在 hyperfixedpod.com 上。

00:27:12.059 --> 00:27:18.819
One of the most liberating and difficult things to say as a teacher is, I don't know.
作为一名教师，最自由、最困难的事情之一是，我不知道。

00:27:18.819 --> 00:27:22.019
I feel like a good teacher will say, I don't know.
我觉得一个好的老师会说，我不知道。

00:27:22.019 --> 00:27:30.700
Or I've never thought about that before, rather than feeling like you as a teacher have all the answers and can't be unsure of something.
或者我以前从来没有想过这个问题，而不是觉得你作为一名老师拥有所有的答案，不能对某些事情感到不确定。

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:35.059
Or maybe you might just say that, no, what I just gave you, that was a terrible note.
或者你可能会说，不，我刚刚给你的，那是一张糟糕的纸条。

00:27:35.059 --> 00:27:36.059
Forget it.
忘了它。

00:27:36.059 --> 00:27:38.359
You know, I think that's good teaching for me.
你知道，我认为这对我来说是很好的教导。

00:27:38.359 --> 00:27:38.859
Yeah.
是的。

00:27:38.859 --> 00:27:40.660
A humility is important.
谦虚很重要。

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:41.460
Yeah.
是的。

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:49.259
When I was coming out of NYU, a lot of the casting people and agents would always say, the NYU people just seem very authentic.
当我从纽约大学出来时，很多选角人员和经纪人总是说，纽约大学的人看起来很真实。

00:27:49.259 --> 00:27:51.660
You know, they seem like they know who they are.
你知道，他们似乎知道自己是谁。

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:55.259
I think, Deb, that you've been there a long time.
我想，黛布，你已经在那里呆了很长时间了。

00:27:55.259 --> 00:27:57.259
How long have you taught at Juilliard also?
您在茱莉亚音乐学院任教多久了？

00:27:57.259 --> 00:28:01.359
I've been at Juilliard since about 1988, I think.
我想我大约从 1988 年就开始在茱莉亚音乐学院工作了。

00:28:01.359 --> 00:28:01.859
Wow.
哇。

00:28:01.859 --> 00:28:03.059
And NYU since...
纽约大学自...

00:28:03.059 --> 00:28:05.660
Well, full time since 81.
好吧，从81年开始全职。

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:06.740
Wow, okay.
哇，好吧。

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:07.980
I know, it's forever.
我知道，这是永远的。

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:11.180
Well, just to, Deb won't do this, but I'm gonna toot her horn for a minute.
好吧，只是，黛布不会这么做，但我要吹响她的号角一分钟。

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:31.740
If you look at all the graduates of Juilliard since 1988 and all the NYU grads since 1981, the NYU graduate acting program, I mean, you've got a lot of Oscar winners, tons of Tony winners, tons of Emmy winners, like a lot of people who have really shaped culture as actors, writers, directors have passed through your classroom.
如果你看看自 1988 年以来茱莉亚音乐学院的所有毕业生，以及自 1981 年以来纽约大学表演专业的所有毕业生，我的意思是，你会看到很多奥斯卡奖得主、大量托尼奖得主、大量艾美奖得主，就像许多真正塑造了演员、作家、导演文化的人一样，他们都曾在你的课堂上走过。

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:38.339
I do feel that NYU had and has a reputation of turning out authentic actors who know who they are.
我确实觉得纽约大学一直以来都享有盛誉，因为它培养出真正了解自己的演员。

00:28:38.339 --> 00:28:47.539
And I think it's because you have such a influence on the department, Deb, that you yourself know who you are and you're very authentic.
我认为这是因为你对部门有如此大的影响力，黛布，你自己知道你是谁，而且你非常真实。

00:28:47.539 --> 00:28:56.740
There's a great quote I read about parenting, but I think it's true for teaching that said, do not prepare the path for the child, prepare the child for the path.
我读过一句关于养育子女的名言，但我认为这对于教学来说是正确的，那就是，不要为孩子准备道路，而要让孩子为这条道路做好准备。

00:28:56.740 --> 00:29:05.019
You are not someone who was giving us easy answers or like you were saying like, look, this stuff is hard, it's vulnerable, it requires risk.
你不是一个给我们简单答案的人，或者像你说的那样，看，这东西很难，它很脆弱，它需要风险。

00:29:05.019 --> 00:29:11.900
I can't get you there, but you would sit with us and observe us and maybe rub our shoulders while we tried.
我无法带你去那里，但你会和我们坐在一起观察我们，也许在我们尝试的时候抚摸我们的肩膀。

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:22.980
Can you talk a little of how this platform that Deb laid for you, the work that she did for you, how you take that to the rest of life outside of acting and music?
你能谈谈黛布如何为你奠定的平台，她为你所做的工作，你如何将其运用到表演和音乐之外的其他生活中？

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:27.339
Well, I realize this Deb at Zelda Fitchanler's Memorial.
嗯，我在塞尔达·菲钱勒纪念馆认识了这个黛布。

00:29:27.339 --> 00:29:31.500
Zelda Fitchanler was this towering figure in American theater in the 20th century.
塞尔达·菲钱勒 (Zelda Fitchanler) 是 20 世纪美国戏剧界的一位杰出人物。

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:32.700
She started arena stage.
她开始了竞技场舞台。

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:36.460
she effectively started the regional theater movement in America.
她有效地发起了美国的地区戏剧运动。

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:47.789
And she ran NYU from the 80s up until, I don't know when she stopped being the chair, but she was really at her, the zenith, when I was there 96 to 99.
她从 80 年代开始管理纽约大学，直到我不知道她什么时候不再担任主席，但当我从 96 岁到 99 岁的时候，她确实处于巅峰状态。

00:29:47.789 --> 00:29:58.670
And she had such strong ideas about the artist, I think she called it the actor citizen or the artist citizen, that people were really hurting emotionally.
她对艺术家有如此强烈的想法，我认为她称之为演员公民或艺术家公民，人们真的在情感上受到了伤害。

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:06.390
And our culture did not provide a space for people to emotionally process, emotionally be vulnerable.
我们的文化没有为人们提供情感处理、情感脆弱的空间。

00:30:06.849 --> 00:30:13.869
She imbued us all with a feeling that we were doing something consequential for the collective.
她让我们所有人都感觉到我们正在为集体做一些有意义的事情。

00:30:13.869 --> 00:30:15.630
That was what NYU felt like at the time.
这就是纽约大学当时的感受。

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:20.670
Deb was squarely in that and felt very much a part of helping me do that.
Deb 完全参与其中，并且觉得自己在帮助我做到这一点上发挥了很大的作用。

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:26.509
So it never felt like I was doing something silly or inconsequential.
所以我从来没有觉得我在做一些愚蠢或无关紧要的事情。

00:30:26.509 --> 00:30:30.630
It felt like I was being lit up with some sense of mission.
感觉就像我被某种使命感所照亮。

00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:33.190
I hope I'm not overstating that, but that's what it felt like.
我希望我没有夸大这一点，但这就是我的感觉。

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:37.349
And I don't know if the school still feels that way, Deb, but that was really what Zelda preached.
我不知道学校是否仍然有这种感觉，黛布，但这确实是塞尔达所宣扬的。

00:30:37.349 --> 00:30:50.670
I mean, the way this woman would speak about the artist and the arts was so incredible and amazingly moving and amazingly just made you want to become an artist even more.
我的意思是，这位女士谈论艺术家和艺术的方式是如此令人难以置信，令人惊叹地感动，令人惊奇的是让你更加想成为一名艺术家。

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:58.670
And I was thinking about her as we were talking a little while ago, because I remember she used some metaphor and it was like about climbing a rope.
不久前我们谈话的时候我就在想她，因为我记得她用了一些比喻，就像爬绳子一样。

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:05.549
like if you're trying to move to get higher, you have to let go, to go up, you have to let go.
就像如果你想爬得更高，你必须放手，要向上，你必须放手。

00:31:05.549 --> 00:31:06.589
Oh yeah, beautiful.
哦，是的，美丽。

00:31:06.589 --> 00:31:13.849
And she would say that to get something, to learn something, you also had to let go of something.
她会说，要得到一些东西，要学习一些东西，你也必须放弃一些东西。

00:31:13.849 --> 00:31:17.069
So there was some loss in learning.
所以在学习上也有一些损失。

00:31:17.069 --> 00:31:19.950
And I do think there is truth to that.
我确实认为这是事实。

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:26.910
I just feel so well served by my education there because it was a place of risk taking when I was scared of failure.
我觉得在那里接受的教育对我很有帮助，因为当我害怕失败时，那里是一个敢于冒险的地方。

00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:29.990
and it was a place of vulnerability when I was scared of that.
当我对此感到害怕时，这是一个脆弱的地方。

00:31:29.990 --> 00:31:34.109
There was so much letting go of the rope that I had to do to even stick around.
我必须松开绳子才能坚持下去。

00:31:34.109 --> 00:31:41.589
Even though it felt like life and death up there on the fifth floor at Tisch, it was still safe, even though it didn't feel that way, it was.
尽管在蒂施五楼感觉生死攸关，但它仍然很安全，尽管感觉不是那样，但确实安全。

00:31:41.589 --> 00:31:44.509
I'm endlessly grateful to you and that whole program.
我对你和整个计划无限感激。

00:31:44.509 --> 00:31:46.829
Well, I just adore you so much.
嗯，我只是非常喜欢你。

00:31:46.829 --> 00:31:51.109
I mean, I don't mean to be all schmaltzy about things, but I just do.
我的意思是，我并不是故意对事情表现得很坏，但我就是这么做。

00:31:51.109 --> 00:31:54.269
And I just love- We like a little schmaltz on this show.
我就是喜欢——我们喜欢这个节目中的一点伤感。

00:31:54.269 --> 00:31:56.549
I just love, I just love you.
我只是爱，我只是爱你。

00:31:56.549 --> 00:31:59.509
I love seeing you, I love listening to you, I do.
我喜欢见到你，我喜欢听你说话，我确实如此。

00:31:59.509 --> 00:32:00.549
What can I say?
我能说什么？

00:32:00.549 --> 00:32:02.990
You're just very special to me.
你对我来说很特别。

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:09.390
And you know, I just, I'm so happy to see the man that you have become.
你知道，我只是，我很高兴看到你成为这样的人。

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:13.509
And now I sound like somebody at the Varmid's fob, but you know, what can you do?
现在我听起来就像是 Varmid 的朋友，但你知道，你能做什么？

00:32:13.509 --> 00:32:14.349
Yeah, yeah.
是啊是啊。

00:32:14.349 --> 00:32:15.349
Yeah.
是的。

00:32:15.349 --> 00:32:26.470
You know, all of NYU in some ways felt like the karate kid training sequence where, you know, you're painting the fence and washing the car and sanding the floor.
你知道，整个纽约大学在某种程度上感觉就像空手道小子的训练序列，你知道，你要粉刷栅栏、洗车和打磨地板。

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:27.910
And you're like, why are we doing this?
你会想，我们为什么要这样做？

00:32:27.910 --> 00:32:29.589
We came here to act.
我们来这里是为了行动。

00:32:29.589 --> 00:32:33.069
And then you get thrown on a stage and you're like, oh, oh, I'm painting.
然后你被扔到舞台上，你就像，哦，哦，我在画画。

00:32:33.069 --> 00:32:33.910
Oh, okay.
哦好的。

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:35.230
Oh, that's what this is.
哦，原来是这个啊。

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:38.990
And you kind of have to put it all together, but you are getting this education.
你必须把它们放在一起，但你正在接受这种教育。

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:46.589
But sometimes at NYU, I felt like the scarecrow, you know, in the Wizard of Oz, like my leg was over there, my arm was over there.
但有时在纽约大学，我感觉自己就像《绿野仙踪》中的稻草人，就像我的腿在那里，我的手臂在那里。

00:32:46.589 --> 00:32:49.910
And by the end, you just hope that you put it all back together.
到最后，你只希望把一切重新组合起来。

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:56.450
And happily in my case, I did feel like I had put it all back together, but it can be very harrowing to feel like you're pulled apart in that way.
就我而言，幸运的是，我确实感觉自己已经把一切重新组合在一起，但感觉自己被以这种方式分开可能会非常痛苦。

00:32:56.450 --> 00:33:06.160
Yeah, I mean, I do think there is some idea, I think it's a very misconceived idea that we have to break you down before you can get put back together.
是的，我的意思是，我确实认为有一些想法，我认为这是一个非常错误的想法，我们必须先将你分解，然后才能将你重新组合在一起。

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:08.079
Who wants to break somebody down?
谁想要击垮一个人？

00:33:08.079 --> 00:33:10.559
I mean, that seems cruel.
我的意思是，这看起来很残酷。

00:33:10.559 --> 00:33:11.559
I hate that.
我讨厌那样。

00:33:11.559 --> 00:33:20.920
I hate when people describe training that way because I'm hoping that they are wrong rather than right because I don't want to be the person to break someone down.
我讨厌人们这样描述训练，因为我希望他们是错的而不是对的，因为我不想成为让别人崩溃的人。

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:23.319
I mean, that is just ridiculous.
我的意思是，这太荒谬了。

00:33:23.319 --> 00:33:28.140
And presumably they were admitted to the school for something that was interesting about them.
想必他们被学校录取是因为他们有一些有趣的事情。

00:33:28.140 --> 00:33:29.220
You don't want to expunge that.
你不想删除它。

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:42.200
Yes, but you know, when you were talking earlier about feeling just so neurotic and trying to prove yourself in every class, you know, there are people that to this day think that certain people get taken into a school because they got the wrong person.
是的，但是你知道，当你之前谈到感觉如此神经质并试图在每堂课上证明自己时，你知道，直到今天还有人认为某些人被录取到学校是因为他们找错了人。

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:44.740
And I think people think that about themselves too.
我认为人们对自己也有同样的看法。

00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:45.579
Right.
正确的。

00:33:45.579 --> 00:33:48.299
They just had my name mixed up with somebody else.
他们只是把我的名字和其他人的名字搞混了。

00:33:48.299 --> 00:33:53.500
Well, it's also if you saw the math equation that you can actually hand, that's not you.
好吧，如果你看到了你实际上可以掌握的数学方程，那也不是你。

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:58.289
And you say, this is why I'm here because of this thing.
你说，这就是我来这里的原因。

00:33:58.289 --> 00:34:08.210
There's something about being an actor that is imposter syndrome is a very understandable consequence of being an actor because it's me.
作为一名演员，冒名顶替综合症是作为一名演员的一个非常可以理解的后果，因为这就是我。

00:34:08.210 --> 00:34:13.610
I do know that your class was one that I always felt safest in.
我确实知道你们的班级是我一直感到最安全的班级。

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:17.849
I felt like I could be the most relaxed version of me.
我觉得我可以成为最放松的自己。

00:34:17.849 --> 00:34:23.449
Your class was a bit of an oasis or a respite from that relentless self-criticism.
你的班级有点像一片绿洲，或者是从无情的自我批评中得到喘息的地方。

00:34:23.449 --> 00:34:25.170
endlessly grateful for that.
对此无限感激。

00:34:25.170 --> 00:34:27.170
Deb, what inspires you?
黛布，是什么激励了你？

00:34:27.170 --> 00:34:30.250
You said you've been at NYU since 1981, right?
你说你从 1981 年就开始在纽约大学工作了，对吗？

00:34:30.250 --> 00:34:31.170
So...
所以...

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:39.809
I mean, what really inspires me is just some person that you can give a whole sort of world to of music and singing and art.
我的意思是，真正激励我的只是一个可以赋予音乐、歌唱和艺术整个世界的人。

00:34:39.809 --> 00:34:55.570
I mean, there are just moments in the classroom that are very, very, very moving and very invigorating because you see someone do something they couldn't do before where you see somebody step into something fully in a way that they would tiptoe in before.
我的意思是，课堂上的某些时刻是非常非常非常令人感动和充满活力的，因为你看到有人做了他们以前做不到的事情，你看到有人以他们以前会踮着脚尖的方式完全进入某件事。

00:34:55.570 --> 00:35:03.289
And no two classes are the same and no two students are the same, sometimes it's chaotic or there's a lot of discontent.
没有两个班级是相同的，也没有两个学生是相同的，有时会很混乱或者有很多不满。

00:35:03.289 --> 00:35:11.090
But even with that, there are just great moments of art because I get to work at two places that really put out some amazing actors.
但即便如此，还是有一些伟大的艺术时刻，因为我在两个地方工作，确实培养了一些出色的演员。

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:18.210
You get to see people really early on as they're kind of finding their way in their craft.
你很早就能看到人们，因为他们在自己的技艺中找到了自己的道路。

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:24.750
And it's beautiful to see people as they kind of move through training and move through their careers?
看到人们通过培训和职业生涯发展是很美好的吗？

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:31.369
Do you remember that moment or a collection of moments where you said, I really wanna turn in this direction also?
你还记得你说的那一刻或一系列时刻，我也真的想朝这个方向转变吗？

00:35:31.369 --> 00:35:44.090
Obviously you haven't left anything behind the writing, directing and acting, but where music became more important to you and why that might be and can you tie it back to your music.
显然，你在写作、导演和表演方面没有留下任何东西，但是音乐对你来说变得更加重要，为什么会这样，你能把它和你的音乐联系起来吗？

00:35:44.090 --> 00:35:48.889
Well, music has always been important to me, like it's incredibly important to me.
嗯，音乐对我来说一直很重要，就像它对我来说非常重要一样。

00:35:48.889 --> 00:35:59.530
I was a very serious listener of music, and I went down to the Bexley Public Library and I rented original cast albums, and I read the liner notes.
我是一个非常认真的音乐听众，我去了贝克斯利公共图书馆，租了原版演员专辑，并阅读了内页注释。

00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:02.329
That's a doorway into another world, right?
那是通往另一个世界的大门吧？

00:36:02.329 --> 00:36:03.489
That you can find.
你可以找到。

00:36:03.489 --> 00:36:08.449
So my first time on stage was in musicals, you know?
所以我第一次登上舞台是在音乐剧中，你知道吗？

00:36:08.449 --> 00:36:14.730
When I got to NYU, like I said, I knew while these musicals, it was a point of connection with me and Deb.
当我到达纽约大学时，就像我说的，我知道这些音乐剧是我和黛布之间的联系点。

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:25.610
And I think I just take, as a songwriter, Even if it's me and a guitar, I'm still performing them as a storyteller more than a singer.
我想我只是认为，作为一名歌曲作者，即使是我和一把吉他，我仍然是作为一个讲故事的人而不是歌手来表演它们。

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:27.610
And that is something I got from Deb.
这是我从黛布那里得到的。

00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:29.090
What are you saying?
你在说什么？

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:30.409
What are you trying to get across?
你想传达什么信息？

00:36:30.409 --> 00:36:31.849
What is the urgency of the story?
故事的紧迫性是什么？

00:36:31.849 --> 00:36:33.010
Why do you need to say it?
为什么需要说出来呢？

00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:35.530
Why is this night different all the other nights?
为什么今晚与其他夜晚不同？

00:36:35.530 --> 00:36:38.250
That all comes back to NYU and comes back to Deb.
这一切都回到了纽约大学，也回到了黛布身上。

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:42.289
That technical prowess and perfection is not ever the goal.
技术实力和完美永远不是目标。

00:36:42.289 --> 00:36:44.650
The goal is, does this move me?
目标是，这能打动我吗？

00:36:44.650 --> 00:36:49.929
When you look up and you would see Deb Lapidus be moved, you knew something had happened.
当你抬头看到黛布·拉皮德斯 (Deb Lapidus) 被感动时，你就知道发生了什么事。

00:36:49.929 --> 00:36:54.250
And also, you know, playing the smartest people in the room, like she is the smartest people in the room.
而且，你知道，扮演房间里最聪明的人，就像她是房间里最聪明的人一样。

00:36:54.250 --> 00:36:55.809
Nothing gets by her.
没有什么能瞒过她。

00:36:55.809 --> 00:37:09.050
I just think having a formative mentoring guide at that vulnerable place, when I knew I wanted to say something, but I didn't quite know what it was yet, but I started to get some sense that if I told the truth, that was enough.
我只是认为在那个脆弱的地方有一个形成性的指导指南，当我知道我想说些什么时，但我不太知道它是什么，但我开始感觉到，如果我说实话，那就足够了。

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:10.969
And I've never stopped going back to that.
我从未停止过回顾这一点。

00:37:10.969 --> 00:37:12.690
So, thanks, Deb.
所以，谢谢，黛布。

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:13.929
Thanks, Josh.
谢谢，乔什。

00:37:13.929 --> 00:37:14.769
This is great.
这太棒了。

00:37:14.769 --> 00:37:16.489
I'm feeling really good today.
我今天感觉非常好。

00:37:16.489 --> 00:37:21.349
I have to go teach in a little bit, and I'm actually really inspired from this conversation.
我得去教一点课，实际上我从这次谈话中得到了很大的启发。

00:37:21.349 --> 00:37:30.710
I think it's going to change how I teach today.
我认为这将改变我今天的教学方式。

00:37:30.710 --> 00:37:37.429
How was it to hear your friend and artist collaborator in this context?
听到你的朋友和艺术家合作者在这种情况下感觉如何？

00:37:37.429 --> 00:37:46.869
I mean, what's so cool is Josh is constantly reaching for mentors in artistic, personal, spiritual, and he's always bringing them to you.
我的意思是，最酷的是乔什不断地寻求艺术、个人、精神方面的导师，他总是把他们带给你。

00:37:46.869 --> 00:37:48.070
And he does it in the interview.
他在采访中也这么做了。

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:56.949
He quotes one, he has all these things in his head, teachers that either he had personally or just people who he considers to be teachers.
他引用了一个说法，他脑子里有所有这些东西，老师要么是他个人的老师，要么只是他认为是老师的人。

00:37:56.949 --> 00:37:59.190
And he wants to share that constantly.
他想不断地分享这一点。

00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:00.449
I love it, I love it.
我喜欢它，我喜欢它。

00:38:00.449 --> 00:38:13.429
And something that I am loving about this adventure that we're on for episodes in is that we have found mentor, artists, relationships at all stages of people's lives.
我喜欢我们在剧集中进行的这次冒险的一点是，我们在人们生活的各个阶段找到了导师、艺术家和人际关系。

00:38:13.429 --> 00:38:20.110
So when we talked to Lynn and Bobby, we talked to Barbara Ames, who taught us when we were 10, even younger.
因此，当我们与 Lynn 和 Bobby 交谈时，我们也与 Barbara Ames 交谈，她在我们 10 岁甚至更年轻的时候就教过我们。

00:38:20.110 --> 00:38:32.789
And when we talked to Sharifei and Bill Strickland, Sharife talked a lot about his time as a teenager and in his 20s and Bill's mentorship of him in that formative part of his life.
当我们与 Sharifi 和 Bill Strickland 交谈时，Sharife 谈到了他十几岁和 20 多岁的时光，以及比尔在他人生的那个阶段对他的指导。

00:38:32.789 --> 00:38:41.949
Deb and Josh talking about the graduate school mentor relationship, which is a holy other kind of relationship.
黛布和乔什谈论研究生院的导师关系，这是一种神圣的另一种关系。

00:38:41.949 --> 00:38:56.750
This is one of the great gifts, I think, of bringing mentors and artists together is to identify these different places in people's artistic lives, where they've been able to develop and share these relationships.
我认为，将导师和艺术家聚集在一起的伟大礼物之一就是确定人们艺术生活中的这些不同位置，在那里他们能够发展和分享这些关系。

00:38:56.750 --> 00:39:00.949
Fantastic, but you cannot avoid three questions no matter how much you talk.
太棒了，但是无论你讲多少，你都无法避免三个问题。

00:39:00.949 --> 00:39:05.690
So we're gonna wrap up today, as we always do, with three things, one question.
因此，我们将像往常一样，以三件事、一个问题来结束今天的讨论。

00:39:05.690 --> 00:39:06.949
Eric, I have a question for you.
埃里克，我有个问题要问你。

00:39:06.949 --> 00:39:13.710
What are three things you would tell your daughter about how her father met her mother?
关于她父亲如何认识她母亲，您会告诉您女儿哪三件事？

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:15.670
We sang karaoke together.
我们一起唱卡拉OK。

00:39:15.670 --> 00:39:16.710
One thing.
一件事。

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:25.670
We took a research trip to the south side of Chicago, so he could introduce me to the elementary school principal he was studying.
我们去了芝加哥南部进行了一次考察，这样他就可以向我介绍他正在学习的小学校长。

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:26.510
Two.
二。

00:39:26.510 --> 00:39:34.309
We went to my favorite diner, which is no longer there, on the north side of Chicago, and eight cheese fries.
我们去了芝加哥北边我最喜欢的餐馆，现在已经不存在了，吃了八块芝士薯条。

00:39:34.309 --> 00:39:36.949
Wait, Erica, I know if I'm allowed to ask the question, but I'm going to.
等等，艾丽卡，我知道我是否可以问这个问题，但我还是要问。

00:39:36.949 --> 00:39:38.070
What was the name of the diner?
食客叫什么名字？

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:39.469
I don't remember.
我不记得了。

00:39:39.469 --> 00:39:41.630
Tell us about it, maybe one of our listeners knows.
告诉我们吧，也许我们的一位听众知道。

00:39:41.630 --> 00:39:42.829
Oh boy, okay.
噢，孩子，好吧。

00:39:42.829 --> 00:39:44.869
It was in Ravenswood.
那是在雷文斯伍德。

00:39:44.869 --> 00:39:49.010
It was off of the corner of Wilson and Ravenswood.
它位于威尔逊和雷文斯伍德的拐角处。

00:39:49.010 --> 00:39:56.949
It was one of these late-night jobbies, but it wasn't a Greek diner style.
这是深夜工作之一，但不是希腊餐馆的风格。

00:39:56.949 --> 00:40:04.429
For any of my Playmakers Lab friends, it was near where our offices in the mid-2000s was.
对于我的 Playmakers Lab 朋友来说，这里离我们 2000 年代中期的办公室很近。

00:40:05.789 --> 00:40:06.630
Excellent.
出色的。

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:09.630
If you know what she's talking about and you were not allowed to look it up, you just have to know it.
如果你知道她在说什么，但又不允许你查，你就必须知道。

00:40:09.630 --> 00:40:14.190
